<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>In the example above, <m>f(k+1) = f(k) + \left(3 \times 4^k + 15\right)</m>, and a second induction (the lemma) showed that <m>3 \times 4^k + 15</m> is always a multiple of 9. Use the same method for</p>
<p><me>g(n) = {4}^n + {6}n - 1,</me></p>
<p>which is a multiple of <m>{9}</m> for every positive integer <m>n</m>.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Find <m>h(k)</m> such that <m>g(k+1) = g(k) + h(k)</m>.</p>
<div><m>h(k) =</m> <fillin characters="15" name="ans1"/>  </div></li>
<li><p>The lemma you would need says that <m>h(j)</m> is a multiple of <m>{9}</m> for every positive integer <m>j</m>. Its induction step writes <m>h(t+1) = {4} \times h(t) - N</m>, where <m>N</m> is a number. Find <m>N</m>.</p>
<div><m>N =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/>  </div></li>
</ol></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The method needs a lemma because <m>g(k+1) - g(k)</m> still contains a power of <m>{4}</m>, so it needs an induction of its own.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Work out <m>g(k+1) - g(k)</m> term by term. Since <m>{4}^{k+1} = {4} \times {4}^k</m>, we have <m>{4}^{k+1} - {4}^k = {3} \times {4}^k</m>, and <m>{6}(k+1) - {6}k = {6}</m>. So</p>
<p><me>h(k) = {3} \times {4}^k + {6}.</me></p></li>
<li><p>The lemma's base case is <m>h(1) = {3} \times {4} + {6} = {18} = {9} \times {2}</m>. For its induction step, <m>{4} \times h(t) = {3} \times {4}^{t+1} + {24}</m>, while <m>h(t+1) = {3} \times {4}^{t+1} + {6}</m>. So</p>
<p><me>h(t+1) = {4} \times h(t) - {18},</me></p>
<p>and <m>N = {18} = {9} \times {2}</m>, a multiple of <m>{9}</m>. If <m>h(t) = {9}s</m> for some integer <m>s</m>, then <m>h(t+1) = {4} \times {9}s - {18} = {9}\left({4}s - {2}\right)</m>, which is a multiple of <m>{9}</m>.</p>
<p>Then, as in the example, if <m>g(k) = {9}m</m>, the lemma with <m>j = k</m> gives <m>h(k) = {9}s</m>, and <m>g(k+1) = {9}m + {9}s = {9}(m + s)</m>.</p></li>
</ol></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>3\times 4^{k}+6</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>18</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>